From ca1da23b85e9efc1ac5605d3c91cd96150b692ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:15:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/49] x86 peephole: narrow a zero-extended byte compare
 against an imm < 256 back to cmpb

The simplify sole-reader guard keeps a widened byte value in a 32-bit
register, so 'a < 0x80' on a loaded byte became cmpl $128,%r10d (7 bytes)
where main emitted cmpb $-128,%r10b (4 bytes).  After register allocation
narrow_zero_ext_imm_cmp rewrites cmpl $imm,%r32 to cmpb $imm8,%r8 when the
register's last write is a movzbl and every flag consumer reads only ZF/CF:
with the upper 24 bits zero and imm < 256, ZF and CF are identical for both
widths.  Bytes only: a cmpw imm16 form would take a length-changing-prefix
stall.  Skippable as CCC_PEEPHOLE_SKIP=zero_ext_imm_cmp.
---
 .../codegen/peephole/passes/load_op_fuse.rs   | 175 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../x86/codegen/peephole/passes/mod.rs        |   5 +
 2 files changed, 180 insertions(+)

