From a81b79065d87b3ec6f9aba7b5cb546fe18161d65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:16:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/49] linker: -b/--format is GNU ld's input format (binary
 inputs embedded), -Wl, file operands are linked, lccc-ld forwards the
 execstack switches

* -b FORMAT / --format FORMAT name the format of the input files that
  FOLLOW (ld --help: "Specify target for following input files"); #1031
  (args.rs) and the standalone front end mapped -b to --oformat, so the
  embedding idiom produced a flat image. bfd links gcc main.c
  -Wl,-b,binary by reading the trailing libraries as raw data and fails.
  New linker_common::binary_input: ELF/default words need nothing (inputs
  are recognised by content), binary inputs become the object bfd's
  binary target presents (.data WA align 1, _binary_<name>_{start,end}
  and SHN_ABS _size, bfd's name mangling and symbol order), other formats
  are refused. Converted at the two front ends (driver link entry,
  lccc-ld) so the x86-64, i686, AArch64, RISC-V and -r loaders are
  unchanged; a binary state left open before the driver's trailing
  inputs, or a -l under it, is refused. -build-id/-bss-plt stay long
  options (getopt_long_only), not -b uild-id.
* A file named inside -Wl, (gcc m.c -Wl,h.o) was dropped by the shared
  parser and the AArch64 static path, leaving its symbols undefined; it
  is now linked in place. Values of value-taking options are never
  inputs (unit test over every listed option).
* lccc-ld classified --warn-execstack/--no-warn-execstack as benign and
  dropped them, and rejected --warn-execstack-objects/--[no-]error-
  execstack, so the raw linker diverged from the -Wl, path; all five are
  forwarded.

Tests: binary_input_matches_bfd (-r object = bfd's: nm, .data bytes and
flags; driver embeds x86-64 + i686, three spellings, print what the bfd
link prints), exec_stack_report_matches_bfd (8 switch combinations: report
lines, status, PT_GNU_STACK = bfd), #1031's -b alias cases now assert
bfd's refusal; unit tests for the wrapper object, mangling, switch
parsing, driver rewrite and -Wl, operands. RISC-V and AArch64 embeds run
under qemu and match the GNU cross toolchains.
