From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 23:38:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/49] loop_idiom: header copies are bound + 1 elements,
 exit-on-true headers are refused, zero-trip guards skip the loop

Two pre-existing miscompiles at -O2 (differential against gcc-16 and
clang-23 in tests/regression/loop_idiom_zero_trip.c):

* A single-block copy loop runs its copy on the header visit whose
  `iv <u bound` test exits, so `do d[i] = s[i]; while (i++ < n);` copies
  n + 1 bytes; it became memcpy(d, s, n). The rewrites now use
  zext(bound) + 1 in size_t (UINT32_MAX + 1 is a real length on LP64; a
  constant that does not fit is refused before mutation). Such a loop
  dereferences both pointers before its first test, so it needs no
  nullable-pointer zero guard. Live-outs are unchanged: the IV phi exits
  holding `bound`, a bump phi `init + bound`.
* The header check swapped the edges when the true edge of the Ult left
  the loop, then read the test as "iterate while iv < bound".
  `for (;;) { if (i < n) break; d[i] = s[i]; i++; }` copies nothing for
  n != 0 and became memcpy(d, s, n). Such headers are refused.

Code size: the nonzero guard of a disjoint copy with a nullable
parameter is exactly the zero-trip condition, so its false edge now
carries each header phi's preheader value straight to the exit and the
never-iterating loop is deleted (was kept as a "fallback" after
`jne memcpy@PLT`). The overlap guard keeps its scalar loop.
