Subject: [PATCH 30/49] x86-64: a sign-extending widen into a 32-bit register
 result writes movsbl/movswl

The register-direct IntWiden path (emit_int_widen_move, shared with the
PF-15 deferred flush) always emitted movsbq/movswq, also for I8/I16 ->
I32/U32, while the accumulator path emits movsbl. With the outgoing
call-argument preferences a cast result can be homed straight in its
argument register, and the 64-bit form then hid the 32-bit shape from
fold_copy_logical_shift_signext (check_shift_signext_fold.sh: shifted()
regressed from `movl; sarl $24` to `movl; shrl $24; movsbq`).

The 64-bit form is needed only for a raw 64-bit reader of the home, the
contract every 32-bit ALU result already follows (emit_sext32_for_value
over needs_sext_values; explicit widening casts extend their own input).
sext_widen_writes_32 applies it; U32 results always take the 32-bit form
(canonical zero-extended home). The decision is recorded in PendingWiden
so the deferred flush stays identical to the immediate emission.
