From 486f911d64037a314eb2566f7abb5b8d63adf656 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:41:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/49] simplify: narrow a widened compare whenever every
 reader of the cast narrows

The compare narrowing (`Cmp(op, widen(x), C)` -> `Cmp(op, x, C')`) required
the compare to be the cast's sole reader, so a byte classified by several
compares -- Expat's `c < 0x80`, `c >= 0xc2`, `c <= 0xdf`, ... on one promoted
byte -- kept every compare at 32 bits and the extension alive. ms178/lccc main
narrows them all; with the sole-reader rule the shared-latch loop of the name
scan no longer formed and each iteration paid an extra `jmp` (+5.7% Ir on a
standalone copy of the kernel).

The cast dies exactly when every reader is rewritten to its narrow source, so
`casts_dying_under_narrowing` counts, per widening cast, the compares that
`narrow_cast_cmp` accepts plus -- when the widened-condition rewrite is
enabled -- a `CondBranch` on the cast, and both rewrites fire only when that
count equals the cast's use count. One reader of any other kind (sqlite's
`serial_type` call argument) still refuses all of them. The compare rewrite
and the census decide through the one predicate `narrow_cast_cmp`, and the
widened-condition rewrite shares the census, so neither can leave the other's
cast alive beside its narrow source.

Tests: seven census/end-to-end cases (several compares narrow together,
sign-extended compares, a non-compare reader or a non-narrowable compare
blocks all, a branch narrows with its compares, the branch counts only when
its rewrite runs, a both-sides compare and an unread cast).
---
 src/passes/simplify.rs | 573 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 513 insertions(+), 60 deletions(-)

