From 3ecf549ff6fb545eef6527a93f42b5972896ecf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:41:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/49] phi_eliminate: predecessor copies precede the branch's
 compare chain

Copies for a block's successors' phis were appended after everything, so a
latch ended `cmp; <copies>; br` and the compare could not fuse with the branch
on targets without x86-64's compare replay: i686 materialized `setl; movzbl`
and tested the byte (every do-while latch at -m32), AArch64 and RISC-V
likewise kept the condition in a register.

`place_pred_copies` puts the copies ahead of the block's flag tail -- the
`Cmp`/`BitTest` anchor of the CondBranch condition plus its Copy/int-Cast
chain, which must end the block -- when that is a pure reordering: every copy
has a unique destination, no tail instruction defines a copy destination or
source, and no tail instruction reads a destination. A tail read of a copy
source is renamed to that copy's destination, which holds the same value
after the copies. Otherwise the copies stay last, as before. Source spans
move with the copies. This covers self-loop absorption, the uniform-edge
hoist and single-successor edges, which all reach the one insertion site.

Tests: placement cases (operand carried, chain moves, refusals for a compare
reading a destination, a copy of a tail value, non-adjacent and non-branch
tails, spans) and a 20,000-case randomized interpreter check against the
copies-last order (branch outcome and every destination identical).
