From 8fb5adbb4ca154b6d4b5564170943da481c1dc34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:11:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/49] inline: resolve parameter homes through
 param_alloca_values, not by position

The inliner took a callee's parameter homes to be "the first N allocas of
its entry block". Lowering records the homes in `param_alloca_values` (one
per IR parameter, sret included; the backend already treats it as
authoritative), and optimization deletes a home that is dead because the
parameter lives on as SSA `ParamRef`s. With a home gone the positional
rule went wrong in two ways (both pre-existing on main):

* Miscompile-shaped invalid IR: after nested inlining the next alloca is
  the inner callee's temporary (`_mm_set1_pi32` after `_mm_set_pi32`), so
  the argument bridge stored the caller's i32 through that 8-byte Ptr
  slot, ahead of the slot's own definition, and loaded it back as Ptr
  plus a Ptr->I32 cast; the ParamRef-store filter also treated the
  temporary as a home. `CCC_VERIFY_IR` reported 63 dominance violations
  per file at -O1 in mmx_intrinsics_differential(_m32) and
  simd_set_lanes_ubsan; codegen only survived because the backend hoists
  allocas.
* Silent inlining refusal: a callee whose homes were all deleted and that
  has no other alloca found no home, so its ParamRef bridge failed and the
  site was refused -- every such small helper at -O1 (rol64,
  crc32_slice_by_8_step), and tail_call_elim's closed-form rewrites,
  which clear the record (fib).

Now `inlined_param_homes` resolves each recorded home by value id in the
inlined entry block (`None` if deleted); without a record only the
frontend's guarantee is used (homes are the leading alloca run). A
deleted home whose parameter still has ParamRef users gets a fresh home
shaped exactly like lowering's (`param_home_shape`: type, size,
alignment), so the bridge keeps its usual store + load form (direct SSA
substitution of runtime arguments is avoided on purpose, see the
zlib-ng note at `home_subst`). A home behind a non-alloca is hoisted
into the leading run (an alloca has no operands, so this is always
legal); a home outside the entry block refuses the site.

Evidence: 0 verifier violations at O0..Os on all three files (was 63 at
-O1), output identical to GCC at every level incl. -m32. 172 configs
(86 programs x -O1/-O2): byte-identical except the four now-inlined
sites; Callgrind Ir: linux_xxhash64 -O1 521,647 -> 465,979 (-10.7 %),
gzip_crc32_slice8 -O1 12,691,578 -> 12,291,062 (-3.2 %),
gzip_longest_match -O1 -0.03 %, fib -O2 neutral.
Tests: param_home_tests (deleted home with and without a record, home
after the alloca run, home outside the entry block).
