From 6b01e53a3043e7106cdc16995c8a71d29ce2dbad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:27:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 36/49] x86 peephole: fold loads through %rbp when %rbp is an
 allocated GPR

The line classifier has no function context and files every `disp(%rbp)`
load as a stack-slot load (`LoadRbp`), and `fuse_load_into_alu` only
considered `Other` lines. In a function that never makes `%rbp` its frame
pointer, `%rbp` is an ordinary allocated register, so `movq (%rbp), %rsi;
cmpq %rsi, %rdi` -- the fold this pass performs through every other base
register -- was refused purely because of the register the allocator
picked (zstd_double_fast's match check lost `cmpq (%rbp), %rdi` when the
input cursor moved from %r15 to %rbp).

A `LoadRbp` line is now a candidate when its operand is `disp(%rbp)` and
the enclosing function never establishes `%rbp` as its frame (no
setup/restore, leave/enter, rbp-rooted CFA, or opaque line naming rbp;
callee-save push/pop is not frame evidence), cached per function. True
stack slots (`disp(%rsp)`, framed `disp(%rbp)`) stay excluded on purpose:
store forwarding turns their reloads into register copies, which a memory
operand would block. Soundness is unchanged and independent of the
predicate: the fold window admits no memory access of any kind.

Static over 86 programs: -O2/-O3 -1 (zstd_double_fast), -Os -7
(hash_table -4, linux_list_sort -2, zstd -1), -O1 unchanged. Callgrind:
zstd_double_fast 416,839 -> 415,281 Ir, hash_table -Os 7,804,112 ->
7,770,464 (-0.43 %), linux_list_sort neutral; outputs unchanged.
Tests: frameless rbp dereference folds; framed and %rsp slots do not.
