From d6f3c83d017526c56c987d4e3214a4788ce55c77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:29:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 38/49] x86 peephole: fold_setcc_increment asks about the
 rewritten program around back edges

`c += (x != 0)` in a loop lost its `cmp $1, x; sbb $-1, acc` form in
sieve's count_primes (2 -> 4 instructions per iteration: `cmpb $0;
setne %sil; movzbl %sil, %esi; addq %rsi, %r11`) after an unrelated
register-allocation change moved the boolean into %rsi.

Cause: the boolean must be dead after the add, proved by
`provably_dead_lv`. Its dataflow models `setne %sil` as also reading
%rsi (bits 8..63 survive a byte write), so across the back edge the
sequence's own reads keep %rsi live; the uniqueness fallback holds only
while the family appears nowhere else in the function, and %rsi does.
The previous allocation happened to pick a private register.

Fix: when the cheap proofs fail, ask `live_after_dropping_region` --
the query `compare_branch` and the sibling `fold_conditional_increment`
already use for exactly this phantom loop-carried bit -- with the
carrier families neutralised on `setcc..=add`, every line of which loses
its mention of them. The compare stays outside the region: it survives
the rewrite and may read the boolean family itself (`testb %r10b,
%r10b`), so a loop-carried read through it still vetoes.

Tests: the sieve loop shape folds; four genuine observers of the
boolean after the sequence (through the surviving compare, at the loop
header, after the exit, a distinct copy register after the exit) each
refuse. A/B over the 86 programs: -O2/-O3 sieve 72 -> 70 (back to the
pre-series count, Ir identical to it), nothing else moves at -O1, -O2,
-O3, -Os.
