From 4ab54d03696941e63c304fb4370c73f632233e85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:32:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 46/49] i686: one frame-operand translation for direct
 parameter homes

A directly-homed stack parameter keeps its value in the caller's argument
slot, so every frame operand naming any byte of the parameter's frame
extent must address that slot. Only the exact home offset was translated:
interior bytes (`((char *)&a)[1]`), register-indexed bytes (the SIB former
in `frame_sib_for_alloca_base`) and memcpy endpoints addressed the frame
slot that is never written. gcc-16 -m32 prints "2114 44 -16" for the probe
in tests/regression/param_direct_home_extent_i686.c; main printed
"0 44 -1", and benchmark glibc_memcmp at -m32 -O2 exited 2.

* `slot_base_disp` is the single frame-operand former: `slot_ref`,
  `slot_ref_offset`, `param_ref` and the SIB former all go through it, so
  no consumer can translate partially.
* `direct_home_offset` translates by extent: the alloca's data size when
  the parameter's web has one, else the single home byte -- the true owned
  size, never a guessed word.
* `stack_param_direct_home_eligible` returns that extent.

Tests: unit tests for the extent boundary, frame-pointer and
-fomit-frame-pointer forms and the SIB former; a -m32 runtime test for
1/2/4/8-byte and float parameters, interior and indexed reads, interior
writes, escapes and memcpy into/out of a home, at -O1/-O2/-Os with and
without a frame pointer (fails on main with 10 errors).
