From 1666d905ff35f8449f7ade6b1c49ca257690dced Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:32:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 47/49] i686: straight-line memcpy with one window shared by
 emitter and rewrite

`Memcpy` was always `rep movsb`, which pays a ~20-cycle start-up at every
small size and loses even to `call memcpy@PLT`. Measured on the host
(copy, then read the copy's last dword; min of 301, L1-hot), cycles:

    bytes   rep movsb   movups+overlap   movl pairs   memcpy@PLT
    8..64      21.76             4.60    4.60-7.64      6.9-8.5
      128      21.76             5.25  13.03-13.88          9.7
      256      25.25             9.58        26.58         17.1

* `emit_small_memcpy` copies straight-line: frame objects as frame
  operands (no address register), register-held pointers in place, and
  anything else materialized into whichever of %ecx/%edx the other
  endpoint does not use. No callee-saved register is borrowed.
* `straight_memcpy_max(sse)` (256 with SSE, 64 without) is ONE function
  read by the emitter and by `builtin_policy::library_memcpy_inline_max`,
  so a `memcpy` call becomes IR `Memcpy` exactly when it will be expanded
  this way: explicit `memcpy` of 65..256 bytes was left a PLT call while
  struct assignment of the same size was inlined. The pass learns i686
  SSE from `loop_idiom::Capabilities::i686_sse`, resolved by the driver
  from `codegen_no_sse`, the same answer `CodegenOptions::no_sse` gets.
* -Os compares the two candidates' exact encodings (the in-tree encoder
  on the text each would emit) and keeps the smaller, the straight-line
  copy on a tie. A fixed byte window was wrong both ways: frame-to-frame
  copies with disp32 operands lost to `rep movsb` already at 8 bytes,
  register endpoints won at 16 (23 vs 54 bytes against the call).
  The -Os rewrite window stays 64: even `rep movsb` (15-19 bytes) is
  smaller than the argument set-up and call (ptr 34 vs 54, frame 280 vs
  283 function bytes).
* The `rep movsb` address loads are a parallel move. Loading the
  destination into %edi first copied the destination onto itself whenever
  the source pointer was homed in %edi (pre-existing on main). Loads are
  ordered so none clobbers a later one's source, including a pointer in
  %eax against an over-aligned alloca formed through %eax; the exchange
  is one `xchgl`. No self-moves are emitted.

Tests: emitter tests for every endpoint kind and register conflict, the
windows at their edges, an exhaustive in-bounds-and-complete check over
each whole window (1..=256 with SSE, 1..=64 without), the -Os choice against both candidates'
encodings (frame near/far, registers; both outcomes occur), and every
rep-load ordering class; policy tests for each target window. Runtime:
memcpy_inline_window_i686 (23 boundary sizes x 5 endpoint kinds, guard
bytes, SSE/no-SSE/-Os) and memcpy_rep_pointer_cycle_i686 (fails on main).
